Chapter 24 — Part 1
Morning light cut through the high basement window. Curtis sat up first, braids loose, scarred hand resting on his knee. He stared at the unfinished panels and debated the day. Two weeks until the Festival. Train. Work. Or neither.
Hina pushed up beside him. Yui followed, hair messy, sheet barely covering her. “Shouldn’t you train for the Festival?”
“Oh. I’m always training.”
He stood in one smooth motion and jumped. He landed on the tips of his toes, balanced, then flowed into a stretch. Arms overhead. Spine rolling. Simple at first. Then deeper. He leaned backward, further and further, until his chin rested on the floor between his feet, body folded clean, breathing even. The compression pants he’d pulled on sometime in the night strained across the pose.
Hina and Yui watched from the bed. The sight of him that flexible, that casual about it, hit the leftover Free Love still sitting under their skin. They exchanged a look. Mischievous. Synchronized.
Hina reached out and caught his ankle. Yui grabbed the other. Together they tugged. Curtis let them. He unfolded and dropped back onto the mattress between them with a quiet laugh.
“Two weeks,” he said, already being pulled down again. “I can spare a morning.”
They didn’t waste it. The unfinished suite filled with soft sounds while the Festival waited and the day officially started late.
Curtis got dressed after the second round. Fresh clothes from the duffel: white button-down, red tie loose at the collar, black suspenders, dark pants, and the gray jacket with the red inner lining and straps. He set the red Jordan cap on over his braids and slipped on the gold-framed sunglasses. The whole look sat cleaner than the compression gear, sharper than the school uniform.
Hina sat on the edge of the bed in one of his shirts, watching. She was used to the U.A. blazer or the host-club suspenders and white pants. This was similar—still the red and black, still the height and the braids—but different. More street. Less costume. The sunglasses hid the heterochromia and made the jawline stand out.
“You look good,” she said, cheeks already warm again. “Different good.”
Yui whistled from the bathroom doorway, towel around her. “Host-club Curtis’s cooler cousin. I approve.”
Curtis adjusted the cap, expression easy behind the lenses. “Figured I’d try something that isn’t a uniform. Two weeks. Might as well.”
He grabbed his phone and the suite keys. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet while Hina kept looking at him like the casual version was somehow more dangerous than the ones she already knew.
The three of them stepped out of the suite and up the stairs. Morning air hit the sidewalk. Hina and Yui stopped at the building entrance to see him off, both still smiling, hair damp, his shirt hanging loose on Hina.
Hina bumped Yui’s shoulder. “You regretting not joining the club?”
Yui grinned, shameless. “Somewhat. You brought him home, so not really anymore. Not only that—we can touch him.”
Hina laughed, soft and pleased. “Hehe, yeah. Unlike the club, he seems uncomfy around women who get too handsy. Which is odd. He seemed totally fine with us.”
Curtis adjusted the cap, sunglasses hiding whatever his eyes were doing. “You two aren’t customers. Different.” He gave them a short nod. “I’ll text later. Don’t wait up if the shift runs long.”
“We won’t,” Yui said. Hina just waved, cheeks pink.
He turned and headed down the street, red cap and gray jacket easy in the morning light. The two girls watched him go, the low echo of Free Love still warm under their skin, while the city opened up in front of him and the Festival waited somewhere past the next two weeks.
Curtis stopped in front of a corner news stand. He lit a cigarette with a small snap of flame from his thumb, took a drag, and scanned the headlines behind the plastic.
HERO KILLER ON THE RISE
Stain’s masked face stared out from one paper, red scarf vivid even in print. Another stack beside it carried a blurrier story.
VILLAIN KILLER ON THE LOOSE
No clear photo. Just grainy alley shots and a police sketch that didn’t match the helmet. Body counts from the warehouse district. No agency claimed it.
Curtis exhaled smoke and kept reading. The Stain piece talked about fake heroes and conviction. The other talked about efficient, unnamed strikes and no survivors in certain cases. He tapped ash onto the pavement, sunglasses reflecting the headlines, and didn’t buy either paper.
The cigarette burned between his fingers. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet while the city moved around him and two different kinds of killer shared the same news stand.
School was out. Two full weeks. The Festival announcement had cleared the campus so students could train, rest, or panic in private. Curtis had none of those plans locked in yet.
He finished the cigarette, crushed it under his boot, and kept walking. The casual clothes drew less attention than the uniform. A few people glanced at the height and the braids, then moved on. His phone buzzed once—Janice confirming he could work tonight if he wanted the hours. He typed back a yes and pocketed it.
The headlines stayed in his head. Stain on one side. An unnamed villain-killer on the other. Both of them his problem now, in different ways. He rolled his neck, the red cap shading his face, and turned toward the station that would take him closer to the club. Two weeks of freedom. A Festival he hadn’t wanted. And a city that was already printing stories about the kind of work he did when the helmet was on.
The low, steady pull in his chest remained quiet while he walked, the morning still early, and the next shift still hours away.
Stain’s hideout was a stripped apartment above a closed shop. One lamp. A wall of blades. The television muttered in the corner.
He stood in front of it, arms crossed, scarf loose around his neck. The broadcast cut between the two headlines. His own masked face filled the screen first—HERO KILLER ON THE RISE—with shaky footage of a downed pro and the usual talk of conviction and fakes. He didn’t blink.
Then the story switched. VILLAIN KILLER ON THE LOOSE. Warehouse bodies. No survivors in the confirmed cases. A police sketch that looked nothing like the helmet he’d seen up close. The anchor called it efficient. Unclaimed. Possibly a new vigilante.
Stain’s eyes narrowed. He remembered the forehead crack, the unmasked heterochromia, the flat voice that had called All Might disgusting and then asked who made them judges. The boy who killed villains and left no speech.
“So they noticed you,” he said to the empty room. The television kept talking. Stain reached over and turned the volume up, the question Curtis had left him still unanswered, while both of their work played back to back on the same cheap screen.
Stain didn’t move when his own face left the screen. The villain-killer segment kept rolling—grainy alleys, body counts, the anchor’s careful voice calling it “targeted” and “unclaimed.” His jaw tightened.
He does not hide behind a speech. He simply removes them.
The thought sat cold. Stain had spent years staining the word “hero” so it would mean something again. This one stained nothing. He left no manifesto. No audience. Just fewer villains who could hurt people twice. The same logic that had stopped Stain’s blade the night before now played out in police reports.
His hand closed around a knife hilt. Not drawn. Just held. Offense still burned from the All Might insult. So did the quieter, unwanted agreement. The boy had been right about the cockroaches. And now the city was printing proof.
“You will not stay unnamed,” Stain said to the screen. The broadcast moved on to weather. He turned it off. The apartment went quiet except for the low sound of the city outside. He remained standing in the dark, the question of whether to hunt the unknown killer or let him work still unanswered, while the scar on his brow throbbed with the memory of the headbutt.
His philosophy was simple. Heroes who chased money, fame, or comfort were fakes. They diluted the title. They taught the next generation that a smile and a sponsorship were enough. He killed them so the word could mean sacrifice again. All Might was the only standard that had ever held. Everything else was rot that needed cutting.
Curtis’s was narrower and colder. Villains who had already proven they would kill or maim the innocent did not get second chances. The law failed. Heroes failed. Leaving them alive was just delayed bodies. He removed them. No speech. No cameras. No claim that he was saving the title of anything. Just fewer threats that could hurt people twice.
They were not the same. Stain hunted the system’s hypocrisy. Curtis hunted its leftovers. One believed the Symbol’s mercy was sacred. The other called that mercy the reason cockroaches kept living. Both ended lives. Both believed they were correcting a broken world. Only one of them still thought All Might’s refusal to kill was a virtue.
Stain’s grip tightened on the knife. The boy had looked him in the eye, unmasked, and asked who had made them judges. The question had not left. Neither had the unwanted agreement that some of the villains All Might spared had later proved the point.
He sheathed the blade. The television stayed off. Outside, the city moved. Somewhere in it, the unknown killer who shared half his logic and none of his faith was walking around in a red cap, and Stain still did not know whether their next meeting would end in blood or another unanswered question.
Curtis stepped into the same café from the other day. The cheesecake case was still bright under the lights. He was halfway to the counter when he saw him.
The man who had come back with the knife. Cheap suit. Same smirk. He was leaned over the counter, too close to the young woman working the register. She had her shoulders drawn in, polite smile strained, clearly uncomfortable. His hand rested on the counter near her arm like he was deciding whether to touch.
Curtis stopped. The red cap shaded his face. Sunglasses hid his eyes. He watched for a second—long enough to confirm it was the same guy, long enough to see the girl shift away again.
The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet. He walked forward anyway, boots even on the tile, and stopped just behind the man’s shoulder.
“You’re in her space.”
The man turned. Recognition hit slow, then fast. His eyes dropped to Curtis’s height, the braids, the fresh casual clothes, and the memory of a knife punching through a palm. The smirk faltered.
Curtis didn’t raise his voice. “Order something or leave. She’s working.”
The café had gone quieter. The girl behind the counter looked between them, hopeful and nervous. The man swallowed, glanced at the door, and decided the cheesecake wasn’t worth it. He stepped back, hands up, and left without another word.
Curtis watched him go, then looked at the worker. “You good?”
She nodded, small. “Thank you.”
“Cheesecake and a sweet tea, when you can.” He moved to a table by the window, cap still on, while the door swung shut behind the man and the ordinary morning tried to settle again.
Curtis waited until the sweet tea arrived and the café noise covered him. He slipped the earpiece in under the cap and kept his voice low.
“Jeeves. Find everything you can on this man.”
He had lifted the wallet on the way past—clean, no one noticed. The ID sat on the table now, half-hidden by the plate. Name, address, a company badge that looked mid-level. He snapped a photo with the cracked phone and sent it through.
Jeeves answered in his ear, crisp. “Receiving. Cross-referencing public records, employment, prior complaints, and police databases. Initial return: two unresolved harassment reports in the last eight months. No convictions. Shall I continue into financials and known associates?”
“All of it.” Curtis took a bite of cheesecake, expression even behind the sunglasses. “And flag if he has any quirk registry hits. I want to know what I’m dealing with before tonight.”
“Understood, sir. Compiling now.”
The earpiece went quiet. Curtis slid the ID back into the wallet, set it aside, and kept eating. Outside the window the street moved like normal. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet while Jeeves worked and the man who had stabbed him walked around somewhere in the city with an empty pocket.
The woman came over once the counter had a lull. She set a small extra slice of cheesecake on his table, cheeks pink, and bowed quickly.
“Thank you. Really. He’s been in a few times. Always makes it weird.”
Curtis nudged the plate back toward the center so it didn’t feel like a debt. “It was nothing. Plus, that man stabbed me the other night. I let him off easy, but I’m noticing a pattern with him. Does he come here often?”
Her eyes widened at the stabbing part. She glanced at his hands—no fresh bandage—then back at his face. “A couple times a week. Usually later. He waits until it’s just me or one other girl. We tell him to leave. He laughs and orders something cheap so we can’t kick him out right away.”
Curtis nodded, sunglasses still on. “If he shows up again and I’m not here, call the police the second he gets close. Don’t wait to be polite.” He slid a small folded paper with his number across the table. “Or text that. I’ll handle it.”
She took the paper, fingers brushing his for a second, the flush deepening. “Okay. Thank you. Again.”
“Enjoy the quiet while it lasts.” He went back to the tea. She returned to the counter, glancing over more than once, while Jeeves kept compiling in his ear and the low, steady pull in his chest remained quiet.
